  "number": "1.7.8",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Do the functions and defined by have corresponding inverse functions? If not, use algebraic reasoning to explain why; if so, demonstrate by using algebra to find a formula for the inverse function.    For any function of the form , one way to determine if we can view the original input variable as a function of the original output variable is to attempt to solve the equation for in terms of .  Taking , we try to solve for by first subtracting from both sides to get . Next, multiplying both sides by , it follows that . Because the cube root function has the property that for every real number (since the cube root function is the inverse function for the cubing function, and each function has both a domain and range of all real numbers), we can take the cube root of both sides of the preceding equation to get . Finally, adding to both sides, we have determined that . Because we have been able to express as a single function of for every possible value of , this shows that indeed has an inverse and that .  We attempt similar reasoning for the second function, . To solve for , we first subtract from both sides, so that . After multiplying both sides by , we have . Next, it's necessary to take the square root of both sides in an effort to isolate . Here, however, we encounter a crucial issue. Because the function takes any nonzero number and its opposite to the same output (e.g. ), this means that we have to account for both possible inputs that result in the same output. Based on our last equation, this means that either . As such, we find not a single equation that expresses as a function of , but rather two: . Since it appears that can't be expressed as a single function of , it seems to follow that does not have an inverse function.   "
